WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.876
Remember the home cook from the coroutines track?

2
00:00:05.876 --> 00:00:10.173
Tonight, her recipe card lists three dishes: soup, salad and roast.

3
00:00:10.173 --> 00:00:12.353
The card on its own cooks nothing.

4
00:00:12.533 --> 00:00:16.085
Then a diner sits down at the table and asks to be served.

5
00:00:16.085 --> 00:00:19.331
Only now does the cook start, at the top of the card.

6
00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:22.173
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:22.173 --> 00:00:25.585
In a Kotlin flow, the cook hands over each dish with emit.

8
00:00:25.585 --> 00:00:30.291
So when she hands over the soup, does she put it down and get on with the salad?

9
00:00:30.291 --> 00:00:31.291
Or does she wait?

10
00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:37.402
First, let's map the kitchen onto code.

11
00:00:37.402 --> 00:00:41.043
A flow is a stream of values, handed over one at a time.

12
00:00:41.043 --> 00:00:45.724
flow { } is the recipe card: it stores the cooking, and runs none of it.

13
00:00:45.867 --> 00:00:49.048
The cook is the producer, the code inside flow { }.

14
00:00:49.048 --> 00:00:52.540
The diner is the collector, the code inside collect { }.

15
00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:55.596
Calling collect is the diner asking to be served.

16
00:00:55.596 --> 00:00:57.904
And emit hands one dish to the diner.

17
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.393
The lesson's DinnerOrder makes this visible.

18
00:01:01.393 --> 00:01:06.874
The cook prints a line before and after each emit, and the diner prints a line for each dish.

19
00:01:06.874 --> 00:01:08.996
In what order do the lines come out?

20
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.813
Two guesses are tempting.

21
00:01:13.813 --> 00:01:19.232
One: all three cook lines come first, as if emit put each dish on a shelf for later.

22
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:25.529
Two: emit(soup) returned comes before eat soup, as if the cook handed the bowl over and walked off.

23
00:01:25.667 --> 00:01:27.415
Here's what it really prints.

24
00:01:27.415 --> 00:01:31.273
flow built comes first, so building the flow ran nothing at all.

25
00:01:31.273 --> 00:01:34.045
And eat soup comes before emit(soup) returned.

26
00:01:34.045 --> 00:01:36.637
The diner ate while emit was still running.

27
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.721
That first line is what cold means: no collect, no cooking.

28
00:01:40.721 --> 00:01:44.842
And a second collect cooks the whole card again, from the top.

29
00:01:44.842 --> 00:01:46.437
Two diners, two dinners.

30
00:01:46.437 --> 00:01:47.899
Nobody gets leftovers.

31
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.304
So why can't the cook put the bowl down?

32
00:01:51.304 --> 00:01:54.872
Look around: no counter, no shelf, not even a windowsill.

33
00:01:54.872 --> 00:01:58.252
Whoever designed this kitchen never cooked for guests.

34
00:01:58.252 --> 00:02:01.883
So the only place a dish can go is into the diner's hands.

35
00:02:02.067 --> 00:02:05.955
In code, emit is a plain function call into the collector.

36
00:02:05.955 --> 00:02:11.787
The diner's code runs inside emit, and emit returns only when the diner's code returns.

37
00:02:11.933 --> 00:02:14.781
Draw each call that's still running as a box.

38
00:02:14.781 --> 00:02:15.983
main calls collect.

39
00:02:15.983 --> 00:02:18.831
collect runs the flow block: that's the cook.

40
00:02:18.831 --> 00:02:21.552
The cook calls emit: her walk to the table.

41
00:02:21.552 --> 00:02:25.919
And inside emit runs your collect lambda: the diner, eating the soup.

42
00:02:26.067 --> 00:02:30.530
It's all one thread, the worker that runs the code, and one call stack.

43
00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:33.611
emit can't return until the innermost box closes.

44
00:02:33.611 --> 00:02:35.623
So the cook stands at the table.

45
00:02:36.533 --> 00:02:38.480
Now let's put numbers on it.

46
00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.748
The cook needs 100 milliseconds to cook a dish.

47
00:02:41.748 --> 00:02:44.737
The diner needs 300 milliseconds to eat it.

48
00:02:44.737 --> 00:02:46.684
The first emit comes at 100.

49
00:02:46.684 --> 00:02:48.769
When does the second one come?

50
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:57.910
At 100, the cook calls emit with the soup, and stands at the table while the diner eats.

51
00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:01.805
The diner finishes at 400, and only then does emit return.

52
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.597
Then the cook needs 100 more for the salad.

53
00:03:05.597 --> 00:03:09.027
So the second emit comes at 500, not 200.

54
00:03:09.027 --> 00:03:13.796
The roast goes out at 900, and the last bite is at 1,200.

55
00:03:13.933 --> 00:03:19.240
The answer 200 pictures emit dropping the dish in a queue while the cook carries on.

56
00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.072
A plain flow never does that.

57
00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:23.911
Now read the rules off the picture.

58
00:03:23.911 --> 00:03:26.604
A flow is sequential: one value at a time.

59
00:03:26.604 --> 00:03:32.055
The salad can't start while emit(soup) is open, and the diner never holds two plates.

60
00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.871
And a slow collector slows the producer.

61
00:03:34.871 --> 00:03:38.277
The cook could serve a dish every 100 milliseconds.

62
00:03:38.277 --> 00:03:42.417
Here she serves one every 400, because she waits at the table.

63
00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:44.620
That has a name: backpressure.

64
00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:49.669
A slow consumer slows the producer down, so values don't pile up in memory.

65
00:03:49.669 --> 00:03:53.372
A plain flow gets it for free, with no extra machinery.

66
00:03:53.533 --> 00:03:56.504
Cold, one at a time, and slowed by the diner.

67
00:03:56.504 --> 00:04:00.992
Three rules, and all three come from one fact: emit is a plain call.

68
00:04:01.133 --> 00:04:02.647
But free isn't fast.

69
00:04:02.647 --> 00:04:07.944
Three dishes took 1,200 milliseconds, where the diner alone needs 900.

70
00:04:07.944 --> 00:04:13.015
The times add up: each dish costs the cook's time plus the diner's.

71
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.652
And careful where you put a stopwatch.

72
00:04:15.652 --> 00:04:18.168
Say the cook wraps a timer around emit.

73
00:04:18.168 --> 00:04:23.007
It says 300 milliseconds per dish, so she goes shopping for a faster stove.

74
00:04:23.007 --> 00:04:25.782
But a timer around emit measures the diner.

75
00:04:25.782 --> 00:04:27.847
The stove was never the problem.

76
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.949
If you want the cook to work ahead, you need a shelf between the kitchen and the table.

77
00:04:32.949 --> 00:04:37.272
Lesson 4 of this track adds one, called a buffer, and pays for it in memory.

78
00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:39.704
Here the picture breaks.

79
00:04:39.704 --> 00:04:42.713
A real cook at the table is lost to the kitchen.

80
00:04:42.713 --> 00:04:48.417
But a coroutine, work that can pause and carry on later, holds no thread while it's paused.

81
00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:55.363
So while the diner waits, say on a delay or a slow answer, the thread goes off and runs other coroutines.

82
00:04:55.363 --> 00:04:57.424
Only this one dinner is held up.

83
00:04:58.333 --> 00:05:00.006
Here's the dinner in code.

84
00:05:00.006 --> 00:05:03.479
In Kotlin, emit sits in the middle of the cook's loop.

85
00:05:03.479 --> 00:05:11.004
Java has no Flow, so the cook calls the diner's Consumer, and accept, like emit, returns only when the diner is done.

86
00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:14.948
So, does the cook put the soup down and carry on?

87
00:05:14.948 --> 00:05:16.875
No, there's nowhere to put it.

88
00:05:16.875 --> 00:05:18.867
The collector runs inside emit.

89
00:05:18.867 --> 00:05:24.520
So a plain flow is cold, serves one dish at a time, and moves at the speed of its diner.

90
00:05:24.667 --> 00:05:25.559
Quick check.

91
00:05:25.559 --> 00:05:30.689
The cook needs 50 milliseconds per dish, and the diner 200 to eat it.

92
00:05:30.689 --> 00:05:31.433
No buffer.

93
00:05:31.433 --> 00:05:33.589
How long do four dishes take?

94
00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:38.248
1,000 milliseconds.

95
00:05:38.248 --> 00:05:44.728
Each dish costs 50 plus 200, because the cook waits inside emit while the diner eats.
